    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d52">

                <pb facs="0116" n="84" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v19/pg84"/>

                <head>52. Memorandum From the President’s Special Assistant (Rostow) to President
                        Johnson<anchor n="1" xml:id="d52-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1967-05-24T08:45:00-04:00">May 24, 1967, 8:45 a.m.</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Mr. President:</p>
                <p>[<hi rendition="#italic">4 paragraphs (8 lines of source text) not
                        declassified</hi>]</p>
                <p>As Tommy Thompson points out in the third attachment,<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d52-f2"/> “the key question is which of the two aspects of the
                    Soviet public statement they are emphasizing in private.” If private counsel
                    from Moscow remains moderate, there is scope for diplomacy here.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed> Walt </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d52-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d52-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Johnson Library, National Security File, Country File, Middle East
                    Crisis, Vol. I. Top Secret; [<hi rendition="#italic">codeword not
                        declassified</hi>]. A handwritten “L” on the memorandum indicates the
                    President saw it.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d52-f2" n="2" xml:id="d52-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
